<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#first-intermediate-period">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">First Intermediate Period</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 2181–2055 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/22nd-century-bce">22nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The First Intermediate Period is the era of ancient Egyptian history between the collapse of the Old Kingdom and the reunification of the country in the Middle Kingdom. Royal authority at Memphis faded after the Sixth Dynasty, and power passed to provincial governors and rival lines at Herakleopolis and Thebes, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mentuhotep-ii">Mentuhotep II</a> of Thebes reunited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> around 2055 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-intermediate-period.jpg" alt="First Intermediate Period" width="320" height="654" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jeff Dahl · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_Egypt_map-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The First Intermediate Period spanned about 125 years, from c. 2181 to c. 2055 BCE, after the end of the Old Kingdom. It comprises the Seventh Dynasty, mostly considered spurious by Egyptologists, the Eighth, Ninth and Tenth, and part of the Eleventh. Several causes for the Old Kingdom's fall are given, some only hypothetical. One is the very long reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepi-ii">Pepi II</a>, the last major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> of the Sixth Dynasty, who outlived many heirs and so created problems of succession. Another was the rise of provincial nomarchs, whose posts had become hereditary and who built tombs in their own domains, raised armies and fought their neighbours. A third is low <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> floods in a drier climate, bringing famine, but there is no consensus.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Little is known of the Seventh and Eighth Dynasties at Memphis. Manetho describes 70 kings who ruled for 70 days, almost certainly an exaggeration for disorganised kingship. The Ninth and Tenth Dynasties arose at Heracleopolis, each with nineteen listed rulers, while in the south warlords dominated. Ankhtifi, whose tomb was found in 1928 at Mo'alla, 30 kilometres south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxor">Luxor</a>, ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nome">nomes</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nekhen">Hierakonpolis</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edfu">Edfu</a> but failed to take the Theban nome. His tomb autobiography claims he rescued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> from famine: &quot;I gave bread to the hungry and did not allow anyone to die&quot;.</p><p>The Theban kings descended from Intef, nomarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>. Intef II began the assault on the north, particularly at Abydos, and Intef III completed its conquest and moved into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-egypt">Middle Egypt</a>. Mentuhotep II then defeated the Heracleopolitan kings and reunified the country, beginning the Middle Kingdom.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Egyptologists Georg Steindorff and Henri Frankfort coined the term in 1926, and the period is often called a dark age. Ludwig David Morenz has argued it was not one and proposed the name Period of Regions. Very little monumental evidence survives. The Theban kings developed a distinctive Pre-Unification Theban Style of relief and built saff tombs at El-Tarif. The Ipuwer Papyrus, which modern scholarship does not date to the period but which may refer to it, records impoverishment in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “First Intermediate Period of Egypt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Middle Kingdom grew out of the divided Egypt of the <b>First Intermediate Period</b>, when a dynasty at Herakleopolis claimed the throne in the north and a line of Theban rulers, the Eleventh Dynasty, built up power in the south.</blockquote><a href="/empire/middle-kingdom-of-egypt">Middle Kingdom of Egypt · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…by decades of famine and strife, the period of disunity and relative cultural decline that Egyptologists call the <b>First Intermediate Period</b>, beginning with the Seventh Dynasty; the tomb inscription of Ankhtifi, a nomarch of its early years, describes the pitiful state of the country when famine stalked the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/old-kingdom-of-egypt">Old Kingdom of Egypt · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The pyramid was likely looted as early as the <b>First Intermediate Period</b>, and Joyce Tyldesley notes evidence that it had been opened and emptied by the Middle Kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/work/great-pyramid-of-giza">Great Pyramid of Giza · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>Some time around the <b>First Intermediate Period</b> the Giza Necropolis was abandoned and sand buried the Sphinx to its shoulders.</blockquote><a href="/work/great-sphinx-of-giza">Great Sphinx of Giza · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>Its brief, obscure history marks the end of Old Kingdom unity and the beginning of the <b>First Intermediate Period</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/eighth-dynasty-of-egypt">Eighth Dynasty of Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
